{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdZB4TMfzRxmmFnJqn6E","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dartmouth announced Provost Santiago Schnell's resignation on Wednesday, and on Thursday the college opened a conference marking the 70th anniversary of the 1956 summer research project that it describes as the start of the AI field. The conference is titled \"What Must Remain Human?\" The article reports the two events side by side without saying they are connected.\n\nThe resignation followed a faculty committee's finding that Schnell used AI to \"meaningfully alter his writing\" and \"was not forthcoming about his AI use.\" The inquiry grew out of Semafor's screening of guest columns in major newspapers, which found his August Washington Post column on AI cheating to be entirely written by AI. Schnell said he used ChatGPT to \"refine a few arguments.\" The student paper The Dartmouth then ran his other publications through an AI detector and reported high rates of AI use in his recent writing, though the article does not say how reliable such detectors are. Schnell referred questions to his attorneys, who had not responded to the AP.\n\nPresident Sian Leah Beilock framed the outcome around the college's AI standing. Keeping Dartmouth at the forefront of AI, she wrote, means \"leading through an environment that is fluid and fast-changing,\" and leaders must \"model the transparency and human judgment that we expect from our students and scholars.\" For a school that claims AI's origin, the statement puts the expectation of openness about AI use on its own senior administrators. How that expectation translates into rules for the many assignments students can now complete with AI tools is something the article does not address."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1360,"cache_read_input_tokens":7183,"cache